    {"text": "Thanks for the recipe suggestion. I ended up making it last night and the sauce was a bit too thin. What could I do differently next time to get a thicker consistency?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["cooking"]},
    {"text": "Following up on the workout plan you provided, I realized I forgot to mention that I have a mild knee injury. Can you modify the leg exercises to be lower impact?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["fitness", "health"]},
    {"text": "Previously we talked about setting up a CI pipeline with GitHub Actions. I have the basic workflow running now. How do I add a step that runs integration tests against a real database?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["devops", "testing"]},

    # ---------------------------------------------------------------
    # LONG  (50-100+ words, ~50 prompts)
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------
    # -- questions / everyday --
    {"text": "I have been working as a software engineer for five years and I am considering switching to product management. My background is in backend systems, I enjoy talking to users and understanding their problems, but I have no formal PM training. I have heard mixed things about bootcamps versus learning on the job. Given my situation, what would be the most realistic path to making this transition without taking a huge pay cut or starting completely from scratch?", "intent": "question", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["career"]},
    {"text": "My partner and I are considering buying our first home in a mid-sized city where the median price is around four hundred thousand dollars. We have saved about sixty thousand for a down payment and our combined income is roughly one hundred and fifty thousand. I keep reading conflicting advice about whether now is a good time to buy or if we should wait for rates to drop further. What factors should we weigh most heavily in making this decision?", "intent": "question", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["finance", "housing"]},
    {"text": "I am a high school teacher and I have noticed that my students are increasingly using AI tools to complete their essays. Rather than just banning these tools outright, I want to find a way to integrate them into my curriculum in a way that still develops critical thinking and writing skills. Do you have any suggestions for assignments or classroom strategies that embrace AI as a tool while preserving genuine learning outcomes?", "intent": "question", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["education", "ai"]},
    {"text": "I have been dealing with chronic lower back pain for about two years now. I have seen my primary care doctor, a chiropractor, and a physical therapist. The physical therapy helped somewhat but the pain always comes back when I sit for long periods, which is unavoidable in my desk job. I have heard about standing desks, ergonomic chairs, and various stretching routines. What combination of approaches tends to be most effective for people in sedentary jobs?", "intent": "question", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["health", "ergonomics"]},
    {"text": "I want to teach my eight-year-old about money management in an age-appropriate way. She gets a small allowance each week and she tends to spend it all immediately on small toys. I remember learning about money mostly through mistakes as an adult and I would like her to develop better habits early. What are some practical strategies or tools that work well for teaching kids this age about saving, spending wisely, and even basic concepts like earning interest?", "intent": "question", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["parenting", "finance"]},
    {"text": "Our family has been thinking about getting solar panels installed on our roof. The house faces mostly south, we live in Colorado where there is plenty of sunshine, and our electricity bill averages about one hundred and eighty dollars a month. However, I am not sure whether to buy the panels outright, lease them, or use a power purchase agreement. Can you walk me through the pros and cons of each option, including the financial implications over a twenty-year period?", "intent": "question", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["energy", "finance"]},
    # -- questions / technical --
    {"text": "I am designing a real-time notification system for a mobile app that needs to handle about fifty thousand concurrent users. Notifications must be delivered within two seconds of the triggering event and each user can have customized delivery preferences like push notifications, email, or in-app alerts. I am evaluating whether to use a message queue like RabbitMQ, a streaming platform like Kafka, or a managed service like AWS SNS. What architecture would you recommend and what are the key trade-offs?", "intent": "question", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["architecture", "distributed-systems"]},
    {"text": "I have a machine learning model trained on tabular data that performs well on the test set with an AUC of 0.94 but when we deployed it to production the performance dropped significantly to around 0.78. The training data was collected over twelve months and the production data is from the most recent quarter. I suspect data drift but I am not sure how to confirm this or what the best monitoring strategy would be. Can you help me think through a systematic approach to diagnosing and addressing this issue?", "intent": "question", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["ml", "mlops"]},
    {"text": "Our development team has grown from five to twenty people over the past year and our monolithic Django application is becoming increasingly difficult to work on. Merge conflicts are frequent, deployments are risky, and different parts of the codebase have very different quality levels. We are considering breaking it into microservices but I have read horror stories about premature decomposition. Given our team size and the fact that we are a B2B SaaS product, what would be a pragmatic approach to evolving our architecture without a risky big-bang rewrite?", "intent": "question", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["architecture", "engineering"]},
    {"text": "I am working on a natural language processing project where I need to classify customer support tickets into about thirty different categories. The dataset is highly imbalanced with some categories having thousands of examples and others having fewer than fifty. I have tried oversampling, undersampling, and class weights but none of these approaches alone have solved the problem. The minority classes still get poor recall. What advanced techniques or combinations of approaches would you suggest for dealing with this level of class imbalance?", "intent": "question", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["ml", "nlp"]},
    {"text": "We need to implement end-to-end encryption for a messaging feature in our healthcare application. The messages contain protected health information so we need to comply with HIPAA requirements. Users should be able to access their messages from multiple devices and we need to support group conversations with up to twenty participants. I have looked into the Signal Protocol and Matrix protocol. What would be the best approach for our use case, considering both security requirements and implementation complexity?", "intent": "question", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["cryptography", "healthcare"]},
    # -- questions / security --
    {"text": "I am the sole IT person at a small nonprofit with about forty employees. We recently had a scare where one employee clicked on a malicious link in an email and it compromised their workstation. Fortunately we caught it early but it exposed how vulnerable we are. With a very limited budget and no dedicated security team, what are the most impactful steps I can take to improve our security posture? I need practical advice that accounts for the reality that our staff has varying levels of technical literacy.", "intent": "question", "domain": "security", "tags": ["organizational", "incident-response"]},
    {"text": "I manage a small web application that stores user data including names, emails, and hashed passwords. A security researcher contacted me saying they found a SQL injection vulnerability in one of our search endpoints. I have patched the immediate issue but now I am worried about what else might be vulnerable. What should my incident response plan look like from here, and what systematic approach should I take to audit the rest of the application for similar vulnerabilities?", "intent": "question", "domain": "security", "tags": ["web-security", "incident-response"]},
    {"text": "Our company is migrating our infrastructure to AWS and the security team has asked me to set up proper IAM policies. Currently everyone on the engineering team has admin access which I know is a bad practice. We have about fifteen engineers working across three different projects with different resource needs. How should I structure IAM roles and policies to follow the principle of least privilege without creating so much friction that developers cannot do their jobs efficiently?", "intent": "question", "domain": "security", "tags": ["cloud", "iam"]},
    # -- requests / everyday --
    {"text": "I am planning a surprise anniversary dinner for my wife at home. She loves Italian food, especially anything with fresh pasta and seafood. I am a decent home cook but I have never made fresh pasta before. Can you give me a complete menu with an appetizer, main course, and dessert, along with a timeline for preparation so that everything is ready by seven in the evening? Assume I can start cooking at three in the afternoon.", "intent": "request", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["cooking", "planning"]},
    {"text": "I have a two-week vacation coming up in September and I want to travel somewhere in South America. I am interested in nature, hiking, and local food. I am not really into nightlife or beach resorts. My budget is moderate, around three thousand dollars excluding flights. I speak basic Spanish. Based on these preferences, suggest two or three destination options with a brief overview of what I would do in each place.", "intent": "request", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["travel"]},
    {"text": "I want to start a habit of reading before bed instead of scrolling through my phone. The problem is that after a long day of work I usually feel too mentally exhausted to pick up a challenging book. I enjoy fiction, especially literary fiction and magical realism. Can you recommend five books that are engaging and beautifully written but not so dense that they require intense concentration? Something I can sink into and enjoy even when I am tired.", "intent": "request", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["books", "wellness"]},
    {"text": "My elderly mother lives alone and I worry about her safety and wellbeing. She is reasonably independent but has had a couple of minor falls recently. She is not very tech savvy but she can use a smartphone for basic tasks like calling and texting. Can you suggest some practical solutions, both technological and otherwise, that would help me keep an eye on her safety without making her feel like she is being monitored or losing her independence?", "intent": "request", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["family", "elderly-care"]},
    {"text": "I recently moved to a new city where I do not know anyone and I work remotely so I do not have office colleagues to socialize with. I am an introvert so the idea of going to networking events or meetups feels overwhelming. Can you suggest some lower-pressure ways to gradually build a social circle in a new city that would work for someone who needs to ease into social situations rather than diving in headfirst?", "intent": "request", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["social", "lifestyle"]},
    # -- requests / technical --
    {"text": "I need to design a rate-limiting system for our public API that handles about ten thousand requests per second. The rate limits should be configurable per API key and per endpoint. We need to support both fixed window and sliding window algorithms. The system should be distributed since we run multiple API gateway instances behind a load balancer. Can you outline the high-level architecture, suggest specific technologies, and highlight the main challenges I should anticipate?", "intent": "request", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["architecture", "api"]},
    {"text": "I am preparing for system design interviews at large tech companies and I want to practice designing a URL shortening service similar to bit.ly. Can you walk me through how you would approach this, starting with requirements gathering, then moving through the high-level architecture, database design, and finally discussing scalability considerations? Please also mention common follow-up questions that interviewers typically ask about this type of system.", "intent": "request", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["system-design", "career"]},
    {"text": "Write a comprehensive Python script that monitors a directory for new CSV files, validates that they conform to an expected schema with specific column names and data types, transforms the data by cleaning null values and standardizing date formats, and then loads the results into a PostgreSQL database. Include proper error handling, logging, and the ability to resume from where it left off if the script crashes mid-batch. Provide the code with clear comments explaining each section.", "intent": "request", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["programming", "data-engineering"]},
    {"text": "Our startup is about to launch and we need to set up observability for our Kubernetes-based microservices architecture. We currently have no monitoring in place. The system consists of twelve services written in Python and Go, communicating via gRPC and a Kafka message bus. What observability stack would you recommend for a small team, covering metrics, logging, and distributed tracing? Please include specific tool recommendations and explain how they fit together.", "intent": "request", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["devops", "observability"]},
    # -- requests / formal --
    {"text": "I need to write a proposal for my company's leadership team requesting budget approval for a new data analytics platform. The project would cost approximately two hundred thousand dollars over two years and would replace our current patchwork of spreadsheets and disconnected databases. I need to clearly articulate the business value, expected return on investment, and risk mitigation plan. Can you help me draft a structured one-page executive summary that hits all these points persuasively?", "intent": "request", "domain": "formal", "tags": ["business", "writing"]},
    {"text": "I am the chair of a small nonprofit board and I need to prepare remarks for our annual fundraising gala. The audience will include long-time donors, local business leaders, and community members who are attending for the first time. The speech should acknowledge our accomplishments this year, honestly address the challenges we faced, and inspire people to increase their support. It should be warm and genuine, not corporate or stiff. Aim for about five minutes of speaking time.", "intent": "request", "domain": "formal", "tags": ["speech", "nonprofit"]},
    {"text": "Please help me draft a formal research abstract for a study on the effects of urban green spaces on mental health outcomes in low-income communities. The study used a mixed-methods approach combining survey data from three hundred participants with qualitative interviews from twenty community leaders. Key findings include a statistically significant correlation between proximity to green spaces and self-reported wellbeing scores, with stronger effects observed among residents who had lived in the area for more than five years.", "intent": "request", "domain": "formal", "tags": ["academic", "research"]},
    # -- requests / informal --
    {"text": "Okay so here is the deal. I have been trying to get into coffee snobbery but every time I try to use my French press the coffee comes out either way too bitter or weirdly weak. I have watched like ten YouTube videos and I am still messing it up. Can you just give me a dead simple no-nonsense guide to making a decent cup of French press coffee? And please skip the pretentious stuff about single-origin beans from specific altitudes, I just want it to taste good.", "intent": "request", "domain": "informal", "tags": ["cooking", "humor"]},
    {"text": "So my roommate and I have been having this ridiculous ongoing debate and I need a third opinion. He claims that a hot dog is a sandwich because it is bread with filling in between. I say that is absurd because by that logic a taco is also a sandwich and so is a burrito and basically everything is a sandwich. Can you settle this once and for all with some kind of logical framework for what constitutes a sandwich?", "intent": "request", "domain": "informal", "tags": ["humor", "debate"]},
    {"text": "Alright, I need your help figuring out the logistics of throwing a surprise birthday party for my best friend. She is extremely observant and suspicious by nature so the usual tricks will not work. The party would be at my apartment, which she visits regularly, so I cannot have any decorations up early. I need a foolproof plan that accounts for getting her to the apartment at the right time without making it obvious, managing about twenty guests arriving without her noticing, and somehow decorating in under thirty minutes.", "intent": "request", "domain": "informal", "tags": ["party", "planning"]},
    # -- statements / everyday --
    {"text": "I have been commuting by bicycle for the past three years and it has completely changed my relationship with my city. I notice things I never saw from inside a car, like small parks, interesting architecture, and the way different neighborhoods transition into each other. The commute takes about fifteen minutes longer than driving but I arrive at work feeling energized instead of stressed. I genuinely believe that if more people tried bike commuting for even one month, many of them would never go back.", "intent": "statement", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["lifestyle", "transportation"]},
    {"text": "After trying every productivity system out there, from Getting Things Done to the Pomodoro Technique to bullet journaling, I have come to the conclusion that the most important factor in being productive is not the system itself but rather having a clear understanding of your priorities. No system can save you if you are efficiently doing the wrong things. The best approach I have found is to start each week by identifying the three things that would matter most if they were the only things I accomplished.", "intent": "statement", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["productivity"]},
    {"text": "I spent last summer volunteering at a community garden in a food desert neighborhood. The experience completely shifted my understanding of food access issues. Before that, I naively assumed that people in these areas simply chose not to eat healthy food. In reality, the nearest grocery store with fresh produce was over four miles away and accessible only by two bus transfers. The garden we built provided fresh vegetables to about sixty families and became a gathering place that strengthened the entire community.", "intent": "statement", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["community", "food"]},
    # -- statements / technical --
    {"text": "Having spent the last decade working in data engineering, I have watched the landscape shift from monolithic ETL pipelines running on single servers to distributed streaming architectures processing billions of events per day. The tools have gotten dramatically better but the fundamental challenges remain the same: data quality, schema evolution, and making sure the business actually uses the data you so carefully prepared. The most successful data teams I have seen are the ones that treat data quality as a product, not an afterthought.", "intent": "statement", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["data-engineering", "industry"]},
    {"text": "The current discourse around artificial intelligence tends to oscillate between two extremes: utopian visions of AI solving all of humanity's problems and dystopian fears of superintelligent machines rendering humans obsolete. The reality, at least for the foreseeable future, is much more mundane and interesting. AI is a powerful tool that amplifies human capabilities and biases alike. The real challenge is not whether AI will become sentient but how we ensure that the AI systems we deploy today are fair, transparent, and aligned with the values of the communities they serve.", "intent": "statement", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["ai", "ethics"]},
    {"text": "I recently migrated a legacy Java monolith to a microservices architecture and I want to share a lesson I learned the hard way. We spent months carefully defining service boundaries and building out the infrastructure but we completely underestimated the operational complexity. Suddenly instead of monitoring one application we had eighteen, each with its own failure modes and deployment pipeline. The distributed tracing and observability tooling we set up after the fact should have been in place from day one. If I could do it over, I would start with observability, not service decomposition.", "intent": "statement", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["architecture", "devops"]},
    # -- multi_turn --
    {"text": "Okay so I have been thinking more about the machine learning project we discussed last time. You recommended starting with a baseline logistic regression model before trying anything more complex. I did that and the results were actually pretty decent with an accuracy of about eighty-two percent. However, the precision for the minority class is only around fifty-five percent. Before I move to a more complex model, are there feature engineering steps I should try first that might improve the minority class performance?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["ml"]},
    {"text": "Remember when we talked about my plan to renovate the kitchen? Well, I got three quotes from different contractors and the prices ranged from fifteen thousand to thirty-eight thousand dollars, which is a huge spread. The cheapest contractor has good reviews but wants to use laminate countertops instead of quartz. The most expensive one includes custom cabinetry and high-end appliances. The middle quote seems reasonable but the contractor cannot start for another four months. How should I evaluate these options, and are there specific questions I should ask each contractor before making a decision?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["home-improvement"]},
    {"text": "Following up on our earlier discussion about setting up a home network. I went ahead and bought the mesh WiFi system you suggested and the coverage is much better now. However, I am still having issues with my smart home devices dropping off the network intermittently, especially the ones furthest from any node. These are mostly smart plugs and light bulbs that use the two point four gigahertz band. Is there a way to prioritize or isolate IoT devices on the network to prevent this, and should I consider a separate network for smart home devices?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["networking", "smart-home"]},
    {"text": "So I took your advice and started the couch-to-five-K program three weeks ago. The good news is that I have been sticking with it and I actually look forward to the runs now. The bad news is that I am getting a dull ache on the outside of my right knee that shows up about fifteen minutes into each run and gets worse if I push through it. I am running on sidewalks in regular sneakers. Before I see a doctor, are there common causes for this type of knee pain in new runners and are there immediate adjustments I should make to my routine?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["fitness", "health"]},
    {"text": "Last time we spoke you helped me set up a basic CI pipeline with GitHub Actions for my Python project. That is working great now. The next thing I want to tackle is automated deployments to our staging environment whenever a pull request is merged into the main branch. Our staging server is an EC2 instance running Docker. I want the deployment to pull the latest Docker image, run database migrations, and then swap the running container with zero downtime. Can you help me extend the existing workflow to handle this?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "technical", "tags": ["devops", "ci-cd"]},
    {"text": "In our previous conversation you suggested several strategies for dealing with my difficult coworker who tends to dominate meetings and dismiss other people's ideas. I tried the approach where I acknowledge their input first before redirecting the conversation and it actually worked reasonably well in our last two team meetings. However, there is a new wrinkle. This person has now started sending lengthy emails after meetings basically relitigating decisions that were already made by the group. How should I handle this without escalating the situation to management?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "formal", "tags": ["workplace", "communication"]},
    {"text": "Going back to our discussion about investing for retirement. I opened the Roth IRA like you suggested and set up automatic monthly contributions. Now I need to actually choose what to invest in. The brokerage platform offers hundreds of funds and individual stocks and honestly the options are overwhelming. My retirement is about thirty years away, I have a moderate risk tolerance, and I do not want to spend a lot of time actively managing the portfolio. Can you suggest a simple allocation strategy and maybe some specific types of funds I should look at?", "intent": "multi_turn", "domain": "everyday", "tags": ["finance", "investing"]},
